From 840721c86d705c1cd1187d88005641960d505181 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 23:18:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 92/93] software_isp: debayer_egl: Bin the raw frame in 2x2
 cells at 10 bits, and filter them

The packed Bayer shader reads only the byte holding a pixel's 8 high bits
and ignores the 2 low bits, and an output smaller than the input takes one
pixel in each cell of the scaling, averaging nothing. In the dark, where the
signal is a few tens of counts above black and the tone curve then lifts it
by up to 16x, the 4-count steps and the unaveraged sensor noise are what
shows.

For an output at most two thirds of the input's size, bin each 2x2 cell of
the frame in a first pass at the full 10 bits (bayer_cells_pack.frag: the
red, the blue and the two greens' sum, packed in an RGBA8 texel), and have
the main pass sample the cells (CELL_PATH in bayer_1x_packed.frag), with
the colour processing shared by a function. Where DebayerParams::denoise is
enabled the main pass first filters the 2x2 nearest cells with a bilateral
filter over their 3x3 neighbourhoods, whose range kernel follows the
sensor's noise: a variance of slope * level + floor, in widths set by the
tuning. On a synthetic scene with the measured sensor noise, a 1920x1440
output from 4080x3072 has an error against the clean image of 1.8 counts
(10-bit) in flat areas at 16x gain, against 6.3 now, and no more error on
sharp edges, thin lines and fine detail.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
